  "slug": "learning-graph-f53d",
  "summary": "Five mid-November 2026 preprints apply transformer, graph, and flow-matching models to single-cell multi-omics and perturbation prediction.",
  "synthesis": "The dominant thread is learning transferable representations from single-cell data by encoding biological context rather than treating cells or genes in isolation. Bloom, a transformer trained on 300,000 mouse brain cells, maps gene expression and chromatin accessibility into shared representations across modalities and contexts. MCT jointly models cell sets from tissues to produce context-aware gene embeddings that capture cross-cell gene co-regulation. GFM couples knowledge graphs with latent flow matching to predict single-cell transcriptomic responses to unseen Perturb-seq gene perturbations. DRAFT combines graph attention with recurrent learning to infer dynamic gene regulatory networks along time-resolved multi-omics trajectories. scUNVEIL pretrains on about 92 million human cells using next-UMI prediction and enriched expression to generate scRNA-seq cell embeddings. The architectures differ, but the shared move is incorporating structure, whether modality, cell-set context, prior knowledge graphs, or temporal ordering, directly into the learned representation.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-19"
}
